WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.000
Model selection is a resource-matching exercise.

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:06.050
Parameter count provides a rough indication of model size,

00:00:06.050 --> 00:00:09.410
but the loaded footprint also depends on numerical precision, quantization,

00:00:09.410 --> 00:00:11.355
context length, and runtime overhead.

00:00:11.355 --> 00:00:15.495
A model that nearly fills VRAM before receiving a prompt may run out of memory as its

00:00:15.495 --> 00:00:17.000
context cache grows.

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:20.610
Leave operating margin instead of treating published file size as the complete

00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:21.440
requirement.

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:23.990
Quantization reduces the precision used to store model weights,

00:00:23.990 --> 00:00:26.400
lowering memory demand and often increasing speed.

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:28.590
The tradeoff can include some loss of output quality,

00:00:28.590 --> 00:00:30.520
especially at very aggressive settings.

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:35.395
A well-chosen quantized model that remains on the GPU often produces a better interactive

00:00:35.395 --> 00:00:39.025
experience than a larger model split heavily across slower system memory.

00:00:39.025 --> 00:00:41.250
Test more than one quantization when quality matters,

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:43.760
using prompts that reflect the real task.

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:45.160
Choose the model family for the workload.

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:49.080
General chat models are useful for drafting, explanation, and summarization.

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:51.280
Coding models prioritize software tasks.

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:55.710
Vision-language models accept images but require additional memory and compatible runtime

00:00:55.710 --> 00:00:56.274
support.

00:00:56.274 --> 00:00:59.075
Embedding models convert text into vectors for search and retrieval;

00:00:59.075 --> 00:01:02.630
they are usually smaller and should be selected for language, retrieval quality,

00:01:02.630 --> 00:01:04.400
and application compatibility.

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:07.225
Do not assume one model must perform every role.

00:01:07.225 --> 00:01:09.735
Context length controls how much text the model can consider,

00:01:09.735 --> 00:01:12.960
but larger settings consume memory and can slow prompt processing.

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:15.490
Set context to what the workflow actually needs.

00:01:15.490 --> 00:01:18.180
Begin with a smaller, well-supported model that runs reliably,

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:23.409
record speed and memory use, and evaluate answer quality against representative tasks.

00:01:23.409 --> 00:01:26.520
Scale upward only when the smaller model fails a meaningful requirement.

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:30.675
A stable model that fits the workstation and consistently completes the job is the right

00:01:30.675 --> 00:01:31.095
model,

00:01:31.095 --> 00:01:32.520
regardless of leaderboard fashion.
